## What changed

**1. [BLOCKER] `time.sleep` aborted the process** — `time.sleep(-1)` reached `Duration::from_secs_f64`, which panics; a panic in the interpreter thread surfaces as `RuntimeError: The interpreter thread stopped unexpectedly`, uncatchable by any `try`. New `runtime::sleep_duration` refuses non-finite and out-of-range values (`MAX_SLEEP_SECS` = one year) and builds the `Duration` from whole seconds plus a nanosecond remainder.

**2. [BLOCKER] `time.format` panicked, and answered `1970` for `-1`** — `timestamp as u64` saturates at both ends. New `runtime::timestamp_seconds` requires a whole number of seconds from zero below `i64::MAX`; a `chrono` refusal is a `Runtime` error, not a silent one.

**3. [MAJOR] every draw was the wall clock, read with `unwrap()`** — routed through `runtime::with_random` over a per-thread `SplitMix64` with a *checked* clock seed, plus `math.seed(n)` so runs repeat. `random_shuffle` is now Fisher-Yates with a draw per step (it reused one reading for every step, so the permutation depended only on list length).

**4. [MAJOR] the two `arity` callers disagreed** — `runtime::builtin` compared `>`, `call_module_function` compared `!=`. Both now `!=`, so a bare `length()` and a `text.length()` fail the same way. Three `stdlib_modules_test.rs` entries pinned the old too-few wording; they now pin the count refusal — same entries, same assertions.

**5. [MAJOR] `text.join` stringified everything** — `text.join([1, yes, [1]], ",")` answered `"1,yes,[1]"`. Now refused by element position and type: `element 2 is number`.

**6/7. [MAJOR] documents** — README/SPEC now show `math.random(max)` *or* `math.random(min, max)` and `time.format(ts)` *or* `time.format(ts, format)`; SPEC gained the missing `### time` section; AGENTS.md §Math/§Text list only what runs, with the twelve unimplemented names named as absent.

## Tests

Ten new `#[test]`s in `tests/stdlib_module_docs_test.rs` plus three unit tests in `src/runtime.rs`. **Each was run against the old behaviour of its own finding and fails there** — e.g. reverting the sleep fix makes the test fail on the literal string `The interpreter thread stopped unexpectedly`; `assert_refused` rejects that message explicitly, since a test asking only "is it an error?" would have passed on both BLOCKERs.

One thing worth flagging: neither BLOCKER can be *provoked* from a test — no Redblue program can set a machine's clock, and no test clock is before 1970. Those are properties of a call, not a value, so `edge_the_runtime_never_unwraps_a_clock_and_never_builds_a_duration_from_a_bare_float` pins them in the source (comments stripped first). Writing those unit tests also surfaced a **third** latent panic: `Random::below` asked for `1 << 64` bits for any bound with its top bit set. No call site reaches one today; it's now `low_bits(bits)`.

No `#[ignore]`, no `skip`, no `allow(clippy::…)`, no deleted test — `git diff` confirms no `#[test]`/`fn` line was removed.
